-- title:
--   Finitely many elements with a prime-local property generate the unit ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring and let $P : S \to \mathrm{Prop}$ be an arbitrary predicate on elements of $S$. Assume that for every point $\mathfrak p$ of $\operatorname{Spec} S$, i.e. every prime ideal of $S$ in the form of an element of `PrimeSpectrum S`, there exists $g \in S$ with $g \notin \mathfrak p$ and $P(g)$. The conclusion is that there exist a natural number $k$ and a family $g : \mathrm{Fin}\,k \to S$ such that the ideal generated by the set of values of $g$ is the whole of $S$, and $P(g_i)$ holds for every index $i$. Thus the hypothesis, which is local at each prime, yields a single finite list $g_0,\dots,g_{k-1}$ of elements all satisfying $P$ and generating the unit ideal. Note that the family is indexed by a finite type rather than given as a finite subset, and that no further property of $P$ (such as being closed under multiplication or defining an open locus) is assumed.
--
--   This is the standard quasi-compactness argument for $\operatorname{Spec} S$: if the basic open sets $D(g)$ with $P(g)$ cover the spectrum, then finitely many of them already do, in the algebraic form of finitely many such $g$ generating the unit ideal. It is used to assemble prime-local covering data into a single finite cover, in the construction of polarisation data for abelian schemes.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_exists_finset_span_eq_top_of_forall_prime_exists_not_mem.lean

